# Subtest: Submethods
    1..7
    ok 1 - 6.c class consuming 6.e role doesn't receive role's submethods
    ok 2 - 6.c class consuming both 6.c and 6.e roles only gets submethods from 6.c role
    ok 3 - 6.e class consuming 6.e role doesn't receive role's submethods
    ok 4 - 6.e class consuming both 6.c and 6.e roles gets submethods from neither
    ok 5 - 6.c class with no own constructors: 6.c and 6.e roles constructors are ran
    ok 6 - 6.c class with own constructors blocks 6.c role constructors, but not 6.e
    ok 7 - 6.e class with own constructors doesn't block neither 6.e, nor 6.c constructors
ok 4 - Submethods
